  "description": "LeetCode 767 restated: rearrange letters so no two neighbours match; feasible iff maxCount <= (n+1)/2, built in O(n) by putting the top letter on even slots first, with the max-heap version, a wrap-point proof and four-language code.",
  "hero": {
    "tag": "header",
    "attrs": {
      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\">\n    <span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"3\">Level 3 - Intermediate</span><span class=\"time\">25 min</span>\n    <span class=\"chip warn\">Medium</span><span class=\"chip accent\">Greedy</span><span class=\"chip accent\">Heap</span><span class=\"chip accent\">Counting</span>\n    <a class=\"chip\" href=\"https://leetcode.com/problems/reorganize-string/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LC 767</a>\n  </div>\n  <h1>Reorganize String</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Rearrange letters so no two neighbours match. It is possible exactly when the most frequent letter fits in the even slots: <code>maxCount &lt;= (n + 1) / 2</code>. Then either pop the two most frequent letters from a max-heap each round (O(n log 26)), or, faster and shorter, write the top letter on slots 0, 2, 4, ... and pour the rest into the remaining even slots and then the odd ones (O(n)).</p>\n"
